    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/493310151/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """Thursday, Aug. 20. 1903. Friday’s Hill
I have left some time unwritten of. We had a visit from Carey Thomas, President of Bryn Mawr, and her friend, Mary Garrett, and two days of really interesting talk. Carey wonders at us for coming to America. She says there are no interesting or free people there.
The “Michael Fields” moved in to my cottage on Monday which Ray and Winnie had set in order, and they remain delighted with it. The Frys are at High Buildings, with Dickinson, and Janet Dodge, with the Puffers at Green Hill. So we are a colony.
The  Sante Conversazioni  have begun. It is Michael who draws out B.B. by her questions and Field by her silent intense appreciation. Today he talked about the emancipation from art he had gained from art. Art has made him see himself in all nature, has imposed her laws on his eyes, so that he no longer """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 222 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1902-03> .

